# .pii-allow -- real-world identifiers this repo is ALLOWED to contain.
#
# One literal per line, each with a reason. This is an allowlist with a written justification: it
# shows up in the diff and has to be argued for. That is the opposite of quietly widening a regex.
#
# Legitimate entries look like: a vendor's public sender address that a parser must recognize, or a
# public support line. NEVER put the operator's own data here -- if it is theirs, it does not belong
# in a public repo at all.
#
# Example:
#   noreply@vendor.example    # the sender address the classifier fixture must match
#
# THIS FILE IS INTENTIONALLY EMPTY, and that is an audited result rather than a file nobody filled
# in. pii_guard runs clean over the working tree AND the full history of this repo with no entries
# at all (2026-09-14: 8 files, 11 commits, 29 blobs, zero findings). The real hostnames this repo
# does contain -- upos-hz-mirrorakam.akamaized.net and upos-sz-mirrorcosov.bilivideo.com -- are
# bilibili's public CDN infrastructure, served to every viewer worldwide, and no rule flags them
# because they identify no person. If something ever does need an exemption, it goes here with a
# sentence saying why, and the emptiness of this file stops being the answer.
